1" style=3D"color:#286ed0;-webkit-text-decoration:underline;text-decoration=
:underline">relentless trade war</a><span> with Canada, challenging its eco=
nomy. The U.S. has also pulled back support for NATO and Ukraine, leaving t=
he E.U. scrambling to shore up its defenses. That vacuum has left both Cana=
da and the E.U. looking for new partners as the alliances established after=
 World War II shift.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,se=
rif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The invitation for Canada to become a=
n =E2=80=9Cassociate member=E2=80=9D of the E.U., made yesterday by the Eur=
opean Commission president, Ursula von der Leyen, shows just how intent the=
se two traditional U.S. allies are on pursuing a stabler future by binding =
closer together. =E2=80=9CWe must urgently reimagine our partnerships,=E2=
=80=9D von der Leyen said in a speech, as Carney sat next her, beaming.</sp=
an></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 =
0 15px"><span>Between them, the E.U.=E2=80=99s 27 members and Canada have m=
ore than 490 million citizens, four of the world=E2=80=99s 10 largest econo=
mies, and a whole lot of desire to find partners outside the U.S. Carney of=
ten calls Canada =E2=80=9Cthe most European non-European country.=E2=80=9D =
E.U. leaders have spent years joking that it could join the bloc if only it=
 were not on a far-flung landmass.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/2=
5px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But yesterday=E2=80=99s=
 announcement =E2=80=94 which still falls well short of full membership =E2=
=80=94 also highlighted the challenges involved in remaking the world order=
 in real time. It may be easier said than done.</span></p><table border=3D"=
